A||||22||Gokart||Useless pipes!||||5||
Z||000000||Gokart||12-18-1999||09:28 PM||||Is it ok to remove those 2 aluminum colored pipes that are not connected to anything. You know, the ones that are bolted against the back wall of the trunk (engine compartment). There ugly and just doesn't look right with nothing going to them!||||reg||5
Z||000001||fiero56||12-18-1999||09:31 PM||||I don't think they serve any purpose now. They were disconnected for one of the recalls, I guess the dealers just left the remains in the cars. Mine has those disconnected pipes also. I say pull 'em.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Gokart||12-18-1999||10:51 PM||||Thanks Fiero56. Their history!!||||reg||14
Z||000003||Bill Osmer||12-18-1999||10:59 PM||||Hey Guys - those "useless pipes" are part of an unusual cooling system. If you look closely, the one on the passenger's side is pointed at the alternator. The one on the driver's side is pointed at the ignition coil. The cooling air is sourced from an electric fan buried in the trunk area on the passenger's side (can be seen by pulling back the carpeting). That fan is activated at the same time the radiator fan is turned on. I wouldn't remove them. In fact, they can be polished and can be made to look much better than they do when they're stock.||||reg||1
Z||000004||TOM||12-18-1999||11:01 PM||||He could mean those cooling pipes or he could mean those two usless pipes that are left after the recall work||||reg||1
Z||000005||fiero56||12-18-1999||11:07 PM||||Why would those pipes be in there if we don't even have the cooling fan. I know I don't have the fan, but do have the pipes. The fan was only used for a few years, on certain models, I don't think these pipes serve any purpose now. Especially if they're not connected to anything. I heard they used to be coolant lines that were run another route after recalls were performed. Anyone else want to comment on these?||||reg||1
Z||000006||HF6582||12-19-1999||12:04 AM||||I have a pipe on the front firewall that has nothing attached to it.<BR>I also have the two cooling tubes for the alt and ign coil.<P>The one on the front has old rubber line left on it but it is cut right even with the end of the pipe.<P>I'm just beginning to think my car was built on a Friday or Monday.<P>Scott||||reg||1
Z||000007||fiero56||12-19-1999||12:19 AM||||Since I'm still going to college right now, I work part time. I've worked at a local bicycle shop for the past two years as their mechanic. It is very easy to tell if a bike was built on friday. The 'friday' bikes are usually damaged in some way from a fall, serial numbers are in odd places, parts are missing, even find sticks of deodorant in the boxes sometimes. Damaged ones are sent back to the factory for in box warranty work.||||reg||1
Z||000008||cobra427||12-19-1999||12:44 AM||||Scott,you must have an 87 Fiero. that pipe you refer to is the breather pipe that was used (now defunked on later v-6's)on the firewall side of the engine. On the 86's, it was used to connect from the firewall side, valve cover, via that pipe to the air cleaner canister through rubber hose connectors.On the 87's, that approach was changed. That outlet on the valve cover was routed by a similar pipe and was connected to the big rubber hose that connects the air cleaner to the throttle body. Rather than eliminate it ,it was left there.<P>  As far as thos other two pipes,try putting on your a/c (which will start your radiator far) and see if thos two pipes don't push out some air. If they do ,keep them. If you don't have a/c, let the radiator fan come on<BR>and then check to see if they push air. If there is still no air, check in the trunk to see if you do have the fan assembly. If you do, you should check to see why its not working.If there is no cooling fan there and you don't like the looks of them, I then say pull them.||||reg||1
Z||000009||HF6582||12-19-1999||12:59 AM||||Cobra427<BR>Its an 86. the cooling fan in the trunk does work. I do have the pcv tube that goes from the valve cover to the rubber inlet boot.<P>It must have been built on MONDAY.<P>Scott||||reg||1
Z||000010||Monkeyman||12-19-1999||01:00 AM||||Just a thought, but if they really are aluminum, why don't you keep them there and polish them?  It would be a cheap dress up for the engine.  Who cares if they are actually used for something anymore?  ||||reg||1
Z||000011||stsmithgt||12-21-1999||02:55 PM||||might as well polish up that dogbone, if it's the aluminum one while your at it.  deburring it helps its looks too.||||reg||1
Z||000012||dogtired||12-21-1999||05:14 PM||||Still Confused?    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>The two alum tubes, In my opinion is a good idea to leave there, for cooling. The ones on the trunk side. There is a fuse and relay right beside the fan. Because our little jewels have the "mid" engine they are HOT, HOT, HOT, and need all the help we can give them!<BR>Now lets talk about the recall. There is a rubber hose, that came off the aircleaner and went to the valve cover? (not exactly sure where the went, but that is close) before the recall. In GMs great wisdom they changed the system? Which left this hose not attached to anything. That is no problem, other than ugly,  but the other end that was in the aircleaner is? There is a hole about the size of a US quarter, in the back of the aircleaner. This hole sucks air directly into your motor (non-filtered) into hole 5 and 6. On high mileage motors you will find less compression there. It is a good idea to plug that hole.                     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>DRUGS, breakfast of champions, and sometimes I think GM.    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif"><BR>Just some thoughts, proceed with caution!||||reg||6
Z||000013||stsmithgt||12-21-1999||07:10 PM||||Got my attention.  I'm looking for that hole in my air cleaner if you were talking about a V6.||||reg||1
